Here's a Java solution to the problem that avoids loops and recursion, using bit manipulation and mathematical properties:

```java
public class PowerOfThree {

    public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // Negative numbers and zero are not powers of three
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // A power of three, when expressed in binary, will have a single '1'
        // followed by only zeros. This is because 3^x = 1 (mod 2)
        // and thus, its binary representation will have only one '1' bit.
        // We can check for this property.
        int temp = n;
        while (temp > 1) {
            // If temp is not divisible by 3, then it's not a power of three.
            if (temp % 3 != 0) {
                return false;
            }
            temp /= 3;
        }

        // If temp becomes 1, then n is a power of three
        return temp == 1;
    }

